1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:01,930
<u>Offline-First Mobile Application</u>

2
00:00:03,377 --> 00:00:04,637
<i>What does offline-first mean?</i>

3
00:00:05,890 --> 00:00:09,160
An offline-first application remains useful with intermittent connectivity.

4
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:14,710
Users can read previously loaded data, make local changes, and synchronize later without silently losing work.

5
00:00:16,209 --> 00:00:18,599
<i>Why can the local database be the UI source of truth?</i>

6
00:00:20,110 --> 00:00:24,870
Using one observable local source prevents the UI from switching between unrelated network and database states.

7
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:29,470
Network synchronization updates the local store, and the UI keeps observing the same model.

8
00:00:30,985 --> 00:00:33,605
<i>Does local source of truth mean the server is not authoritative?</i>

9
00:00:35,126 --> 00:00:35,596
No.

10
00:00:36,296 --> 00:00:40,936
The local database can be the source of truth for presentation while the backend remains authoritative for shared domain state,

11
00:00:41,116 --> 00:00:42,976
authorization, and cross-device coordination.

12
00:00:44,494 --> 00:00:45,704
<i>What is a pending mutation?</i>

13
00:00:47,219 --> 00:00:51,479
A pending mutation is a durable record of a local change that has not yet been confirmed by the server.

14
00:00:52,179 --> 00:00:55,809
It allows the app to recover synchronization after process death or connectivity loss.

15
00:00:57,321 --> 00:00:59,641
<i>Why does a mutation need a stable operation ID?</i>

16
00:01:01,155 --> 00:01:04,885
A stable operation ID lets the server recognize retries of the same logical change.

17
00:01:05,585 --> 00:01:08,705
It is essential when the client cannot know whether an earlier request completed.

18
00:01:10,254 --> 00:01:12,124
<i>Why should pending mutations be persisted?</i>

19
00:01:13,627 --> 00:01:16,007
An in-memory queue disappears when the process dies.

20
00:01:16,707 --> 00:01:22,487
Persisting the visible change and its mutation together prevents local state from being updated without preserving the work required to synchronize it.

21
00:01:24,353 --> 00:01:25,773
<i>What is an optimistic update?</i>

22
00:01:27,034 --> 00:01:30,644
An optimistic update changes local UI immediately before server confirmation.

23
00:01:31,344 --> 00:01:36,174
It improves responsiveness but requires pending/failed state and a recovery path if the server rejects the change.

24
00:01:37,665 --> 00:01:40,125
<i>What happens if the app dies while a mutation is in flight?</i>

25
00:01:41,657 --> 00:01:44,587
in_flight should represent an attempt, not permanent truth.

26
00:01:45,287 --> 00:01:47,337
After restart, timeout, or an expired lease,

27
00:01:47,467 --> 00:01:51,407
unfinished work must become retryable or be reconciled using the same stable operation ID.

28
00:01:52,945 --> 00:01:54,775
<i>How should retries work in offline sync?</i>

29
00:01:56,299 --> 00:02:00,629
Keep failed transient mutations durable and retry later with bounded attempts, backoff, and jitter.

30
00:02:01,339 --> 00:02:04,409
Permanent validation or authorization errors should not retry forever.

31
00:02:05,921 --> 00:02:08,781
<i>Why are connectivity callbacks not proof of backend reachability?</i>

32
00:02:10,278 --> 00:02:15,818
A device can have a network route while DNS, TLS, the API, authentication, or a dependency is still unavailable.

33
00:02:16,518 --> 00:02:20,318
Connectivity changes can trigger an attempt, but the request result remains authoritative.

34
00:02:21,849 --> 00:02:23,769
<i>What is a conflict in offline-first sync?</i>

35
00:02:25,294 --> 00:02:28,364
A conflict happens when local and server versions change independently.

36
00:02:29,074 --> 00:02:32,794
The correct resolution depends on domain semantics rather than one universal policy.

37
00:02:34,297 --> 00:02:35,927
<i>What conflict strategies are common?</i>

38
00:02:37,454 --> 00:02:42,884
Common strategies include server-wins, client-wins, last-write-wins, field-level merge, and domain-specific resolution.

39
00:02:43,594 --> 00:02:46,784
Each trades simplicity against risk of losing user or remote intent.

40
00:02:48,305 --> 00:02:49,245
<i>What is a tombstone?</i>

41
00:02:51,121 --> 00:02:55,371
A tombstone records that an entity was deleted without immediately erasing all evidence of the deletion.

42
00:02:56,081 --> 00:02:59,271
It allows the delete to synchronize across server and replicas before cleanup.
